A||||13||JSocha||Any "mechanical" suggestions for the service tech...||||5||
Z||000000||JSocha||05-15-2001||09:21 AM||||Tonight after work, finally taking my 87GT to a service center in Minot to have them go over my car for "mechanical" soundness (and to see if she is still in heat   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).<P>Any other suggestions to have them look at that they or I would be unaware of would be highly appreciated as I am compiling a list since they may be closed by the time I get there.<P>The vehicle has 62,600 miles on it.<P>I'm having them check the following:<P>Why Engine Service Light comes on at idle speeds<BR>Alternator (VOLT light comes on)<BR>Temperature guage (reads...then doesn't read...and/or points North...thinking a sensor)<BR>3rd gear shifts hard (grinds) unless under 20MPH and I slip it in gently.<BR>Recharging air<BR>Drivers side park outer marker light doesn't come on (even after bulb change).  Thinking it is a bad wire or bad socket.<BR>Rear "PONTIAC" taillights do not work (even after bulb changes)<BR>Checking brakes and brake lines<BR>Suspension<BR>Motor:  Compression; changing spark plugs and wires; having them check altenator; belts.<P>Having them do a B-line as well.<P>I think that would cover it, unless some of you are aware of things that I should have them check with a vehicle having this milage.<P>Please let me know.<P>Thanks,<BR>Jeff<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||JSocha||05-15-2001||12:24 PM||||&lt;struggling grunt&gt;<P>aaaaaaaaahhhhhhh...ah!<P>aka:  "Da Bump"||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||05-16-2001||12:53 AM||||If they are not Fiero experts I would say you are a brave soul...  They'll probably give you a quote for $2K.  It is a pretty extensive check but I would have them change all the fluids including trans & brakes.  Have them inspect the bazillion hoses the Fiero has (mine are oozing coolant through the cords).  Have them replace any bad hoses flush the cooling system and put in the orange 'stuff'.  When they get it together, do a pressure check on it.  Check the waterpump for 'play' when they have the belt off.  Check all of the eng/trans mounts, check/replace the O2 sensor if it is very old.  Have them check out the distributer components and check the timing.  Clean the throttle body, replace the PCV and clean the oil residue from the PCV tubes, check the fuel pressure (running & not running). Replace fuel & air filters, check CV joints and boots, bearings on all 4 corners etc.  I'm tired now... I'll think up more for ya tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||11:18 AM||||now you're up to about $3k.<P>how can you afford to have someone else do all this stuff for you on an older car like a Fiero?<P>Besides, figuring out these things, like the side lights being out, are half the fun of owning a 'classic' car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JSocha||05-16-2001||12:11 PM||||I'm a computer nerd, not a gear head.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Took it to a long time and reputable Pontiac dealer in Minot who my parents take all their vehicles to because they knew the original owner personally as well as the new and it carried over with their technicians who take very good care of my parents vehicles.  <P>Very reasonably price and they don't gowge you for every unneeded part (if that could be proven) like the Chrysler center in Minot does.  Everything seems to cost $1,500 when ever I take a vehicle into them.<P>The service manager who checked my car in said they have worked on a lot of Fiero's (not that many in Minot that I know of other then V8Fiero's) and said his friend use to own an 85GT and he was familiar with the car.  Then he popped the front hood, opened it and looked deeply puzzled then said "wrong release lever".  Reminded me of a recent oil change I had done here in town at the service station.  The mechanic there has now been nicknamed "VW Bug Man" and I have the "VW futuristic bug clone".<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||01:28 PM||||well there you go, you should be right at home working on your fiero.<P>the ECM is the CPU motherboard, and the turn signals and side light and the injectors and tires are the periphials.<P>A Fiero is all digital. The gas pedal is a simple on/off switch (WFO/idle) <P>at least the way I drive it is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||05-16-2001||05:25 PM||||Remember, the "PONTIAC" lamps on the tail lights only illuminate when the ignition switch is in the "ON" position, regardless of position of the headlight switch.<P>The "Service Engine" light could be coming on because of a bad alternator... hint: the VOLT light comes on.<P>Temperature fluctuations... bad sensor.<P>3rd gear grind... bad senchros(sp?). You will probably be better off budgeting for a good replacement transmission.<P>Turn Signal socket... look for corrosion (use a volt meter with a sharp probe) or pinched wire.<P>A/C... Duh!<P>Hope this helps<P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||JSocha||05-16-2001||05:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>Remember, the "PONTIAC" lamps on the tail lights only illuminate when the ignition switch is in the "ON" position, regardless of position of the headlight switch.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They don't go on either way and I'm thinking it may be related to the front drivers side outer marker light or that there is a short in that wiring harness or the sockets themselves.<P>I hope they get the items I listed fixed.  Waiting to hear from them to see what they found and will mention the other items mentioned in this thread for consideration.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||JSocha||05-17-2001||09:57 AM||||Just got off the phone with the service rep in Minot and I need the following parts and said I would check here to see if I can get them.  2.8 V6 w/5sp Getrag tranny:<P>I have a warped manifold exhast with three broken bolts and a burned out 02 Sensor.<P>Left exhaust manifold<BR>O2 Sensor<P>Transmission is hosed, metal parts and metal filings in the tranny: 5sp Getrag (3rd gear shot)<P>Need a new altenator<BR>Need a new interior temperature guage<P>Figured would check here to see if somebody has the parts "in good condition" that they would be willing to sell and at what price.<P>Email me at jsocha@rugbymfg.com or socha@stellarnet.com with prices including S/H.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DJRice||05-17-2001||04:47 PM||||The 02 sensor and Alternator should be available at your local parts housef for approx $100.  <P>The dealer would tell you the tranny was screwed if the sycnhros need replacing.  You could probably have it removed and rebuilt to the tune of $1500 or so.  May be easier to just swap it, but chances are it could be rebuilt/repaired.<P>As for the warped manifolds...good luck getting new ones.  It seems like everyone who doesn't have headers has exhaust manifold issues.  <P>Sounds like a good time to learn how to drop the engine in a Fiero!<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||05:19 PM||||Okay, I gotta say this, please take it with a grain of salt.<P>Ive known several people who 'had friends in the XYZ business, or relatives, or masonic brethern, or whatever' and they could go to them and always know they would get preferred treatment, special deals, taken good care of...<P>but most of the time they were getting screwed over and didnt even know it, cause they trusted the 'special relationship' person completely.<P>Im not implying by anything you have said about your car, or what the dealer mechanic says it needs, actually in this case the dealer/mechanics findings sound about right from what you have told us.<P> But in general, people in the sales and service industries LOVE to have regular customers that come back with repeat business and who have deep pockets. Some times they will treat you fairly, and sometimes they will take advantage of your trust in them.<P>  your best protection is to understand your stuff, your car, your investments, real estate, whatever it is, understand it enough so that you are not operating on blind faith.<P>  JS - you sound like a young guy. I rebuilt the engine on my first car, an aircooled VW, cause it threw a rod and I could not afford to do anything else except take the bus to college (1 hour each way). It was an ordeal at the time, but later it turned into a hobbie and a love for fine machinery.<P>  You cant rebuild the tranny yourself, you need special jigs and tools to do that, but you can start to tackle some of this stuff yourself.<P>  If your gonna have an older car like a Fiero you might as well start doing the mechanical work on it yourself, as much as you can. Stuff like the O2 sensor, I just replaced mine, it was only $20 or $25, and it took me about 30 minutes.<P>  Driving an older or a classic car is expensive if you have to pay someone else to do all the work on it, cause there will be work all the time.<P>  go get some laytex gloves, put some hand lotion on, then the gloves, a tee shirt and some old jeans, and, as an older friend of mine use to say, crawl in the air intake of that machine and nose around inside that engine till you find your way through to the exhaust pipe.<P>  you are paying a mechanic to have half the pleasure of owning a fine machine. If you keep your attitude pointed up, working on a car is a wonderful thing. There is something beautiful about a polished crankshaft, or the discovery that a flywheel rings like a church bell. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||JSocha||05-17-2001||05:36 PM||||"Grain of salt" well taken and received.<P>I just know where my limits (and patience level) are.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||RossT||05-23-2001||12:49 AM||||Ok jeff!  What was the total bill and what did they do to your baby!!!!  Inquiring minds want to know!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||05-23-2001||01:13 AM||||It's still up at Fisher Motors in Minot.<P>Located a transmission for $300.00 with around a 100K on it.  Still waiting to hear back from them to see what condition it is in and as they were going to look at their other lots to see if they can find one with lower miles on it.<P>Got the other parts coming, exhaust manifold, temp/fuel guage, altenator and 02 sensor.<P>Lookin at about $2-2.5K total for the parts and repairs which is better then the 4K I would have had to shell out had they gotten the parts themselves.  Service tech called me to tell me to look around for the parts as they were high priced over and above what he felt was normal.<P>Be glad when my little baby is back and healthy.<P>BTW everyone, false alarm, she wasn't in heat.  Loose screw/bolt causing her to leak.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
